.Dd June 1, 2026
.Dt XTC_INSPECT 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_inspect_procs ,
.Nm xtc_inspect_loops ,
.Nm xtc_proc_info ,
.Nm xtc_runtime_info
.Nd live process and loop introspection
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc.h
.In xtc_inspect.h
.In xtc_runtime.h
.Vt typedef int (*xtc_inspect_proc_fn)(const xtc_proc_info_t *info, void *user);
.Vt typedef int (*xtc_inspect_loop_fn)(const xtc_loop_info_t *info, void *user);
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_inspect_procs "xtc_inspect_proc_fn cb" "void *user"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_inspect_loops "xtc_inspect_loop_fn cb" "void *user"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_proc_info "xtc_pid_t pid" "xtc_proc_info_t *out"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_runtime_info "xtc_runtime_info_t *out"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm xtc_inspect
is the programmatic form of the debugger's
.Ic xtc-procs
and
.Ic xtc-loops
commands
.Pq Xr xtc_proc 3 , Xr xtc_loop 3 ,
callable from inside a running program rather than from a stopped one.
It is the libxtc analog of Erlang's
.Fn erlang:process_info
plus the data
.Sy observer
renders: given the live runtime, enumerate every proc and loop and
read each one's vital signs.  Its consumers are an admin command (a
.Dq SHOW PROCESSES
verb), a metrics scraper, or a terminal observer.
.Pp
Each call takes a best-effort snapshot under the per-loop slot locks.
For the duration of the call the proc set is consistent: no proc is
created or reaped out from under the walk.  The per-proc mailbox
counters are read under the mailbox lock, so they are individually
coherent.  The scheduler-owned run state is
.Em sampled :
it reflects an instant during the call and may change immediately
after.  A snapshot is therefore a faithful point-in-time view, not a
stop-the-world freeze.
.Pp
The enumeration callbacks run
.Em after
all internal locks are released.  A callback may freely call back into
the proc and loop APIs
.Pq including Fn xtc_send , Fn xtc_proc_info ,
or another
.Fn xtc_inspect_procs
without risk of self-deadlock or lock-order inversion.  A callback
returns
.Dv 0
to continue the walk and any non-zero value to stop early; the
enumeration functions then return the number of items visited so far.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_inspect_procs
invokes
.Fa cb
once per live proc, across all loops, passing a pointer to a
populated
.Vt xtc_proc_info_t
and the caller's
.Fa user
pointer.  It returns the number of procs visited, or a negative
.Dv XTC_E_*
code on error.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_inspect_loops
invokes
.Fa cb
once per registered loop, passing a
.Vt xtc_loop_info_t .
It returns the loop count, or a negative
.Dv XTC_E_*
code.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_proc_info
snapshots a single proc, named by
.Fa pid ,
into
.Fa *out .
It is the
.Fn erlang:process_info
analog for one identity.
.Pp
A proc snapshot is reported in:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef struct xtc_proc_info {
        xtc_pid_t pid;
        int       run_state;        /* enum xtc_proc_run_state */
        int       park_reason;      /* enum xtc_proc_park */
        int       alive;
        int       kill_pending;
        size_t    mbox_len;         /* current depth */
        size_t    mbox_peak;        /* high-water mark */
        size_t    mbox_cap;
        size_t    mbox_saved;       /* selective-receive save queue */
        uint64_t  mbox_recv_total;  /* messages ever accepted */
        uint64_t  mbox_drop_total;  /* messages ever rejected */
} xtc_proc_info_t;
.Ed
.Pp
The fields are:
.Bl -tag -width "mbox_recv_total" -offset indent
.It Va pid
The proc identity.
.It Va run_state
The sampled scheduler state, one of the
.Vt enum xtc_proc_run_state
values below.
.It Va park_reason
Why a
.Dv XTC_PROC_PARKED
proc is parked, one of the
.Vt enum xtc_proc_park
values below;
.Dv XTC_PARK_NONE
when the proc is not parked.
.It Va alive
Non-zero while the proc is live.
.It Va kill_pending
Non-zero if an asynchronous
.Fn xtc_exit_pid
has been delivered but not yet acted on.
.It Va mbox_len
The live mailbox depth.  This is the single most useful health
signal in a message-passing system: a large or growing value is the
most common pathology, the same vital sign the debugger's
.Ic xtc-procs
ranks on.
.It Va mbox_peak
The mailbox high-water mark; a peak far above
.Va mbox_len
records a burst the proc fell behind on.
.It Va mbox_cap
The mailbox capacity bound.
.It Va mbox_saved
The selective-receive save-queue depth
.Pq Xr xtc_proc 3 .
.It Va mbox_recv_total
Lifetime count of messages accepted.
.It Va mbox_drop_total
Lifetime count of messages rejected because the mailbox was full or
the proc was dead.
.El
.Pp
.Vt enum xtc_proc_run_state
mirrors the scheduler task state:
.Bl -tag -width XTC_PROC_SCHEDULED -compact -offset indent
.It Dv XTC_PROC_SCHEDULED
Runnable, waiting for a turn on its loop.
.It Dv XTC_PROC_RUNNING
Currently executing on its loop's thread.
.It Dv XTC_PROC_PARKED
Blocked; see
.Va park_reason .
.It Dv XTC_PROC_DONE
Finished, awaiting reap.
.El
.Pp
.Vt enum xtc_proc_park
records why a parked proc is waiting:
.Bl -tag -width XTC_PARK_MAILBOX -compact -offset indent
.It Dv XTC_PARK_NONE
Not parked.
.It Dv XTC_PARK_FD
Waiting on a file descriptor
.Pq Fn xtc_proc_wait_fd .
.It Dv XTC_PARK_TIMER
Waiting on a timer
.Pq Fn xtc_proc_sleep
or a timed
.Fn xtc_recv .
.It Dv XTC_PARK_MAILBOX
Waiting for a message
.Pq a blocking Fn xtc_recv .
.El
.Pp
A loop snapshot is reported in:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef struct xtc_loop_info {
        int      loop_id;     /* 0 standalone, exec slot + 1 otherwise */
        int      n_procs;     /* live procs homed on this loop */
        int      n_alive;     /* live tasks (procs + plain tasks) */
        uint64_t tasks_run;
        uint64_t steals;
} xtc_loop_info_t;
.Ed
.Pp
.Va tasks_run
and
.Va steals
are the scheduler-utilization view: how much work the loop has run and
how much it took by work-stealing.
.Pp
Link and monitor topology is intentionally NOT exposed by the live
API.  A proc mutates its own link and monitor lists without a lock, so
walking them from another thread would race.  To inspect link/monitor
topology use the debugger
.Pq Ic xtc-proc ,
which runs against a stopped program; the live API reports only the
fields that are safe to read concurrently -- the mailbox counters,
read under the mailbox lock, and the sampled run state.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xtc_runtime_info
fills
.Fa *out
with a one-shot snapshot of the running configuration -- loop count,
online / performance / efficiency CPU counts, NUMA node count, and the
configured vs currently-accounted memory -- so a program need not
assemble that itself.  It returns
.Dv XTC_OK
on success.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_inspect_procs
returns the number of procs visited;
.Fn xtc_inspect_loops
returns the number of loops visited.  Both return a negative
.Dv XTC_E_*
code on error.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_proc_info
returns
.Dv XTC_OK
on success,
.Dv XTC_E_NOTFOUND
if no live proc has the given
.Fa pid ,
or
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
if
.Fa out
is NULL.  See
.Xr xtc_strerror 3 .
.Sh EXAMPLES
Rank live procs by mailbox depth, the way a
.Dq SHOW PROCESSES
admin command would:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
static int
on_proc(const xtc_proc_info_t *p, void *user)
{
        (void)user;
        if (p->mbox_len > 0)
                printf("pid=%llu mbox=%zu peak=%zu state=%d\\n",
                    (unsigned long long)p->pid,
                    p->mbox_len, p->mbox_peak, p->run_state);
        return (0);                 /* 0 = keep going */
}

int n = xtc_inspect_procs(on_proc, NULL);
printf("%d live procs\\n", n);
.Ed
.Pp
Snapshot one proc by pid:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
xtc_proc_info_t info;
if (xtc_proc_info(pid, &info) == XTC_OK && info.mbox_len > info.mbox_cap / 2)
        log_warn("proc %llu mailbox half full", (unsigned long long)pid);
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xtc_proc 3 ,
.Xr xtc_reg 3 ,
.Xr xtc_stats 3
